<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Commons of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1341–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Commons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was the lower house of the Parliament of England, made up of knights of the shire and burgesses elected from the towns. It began to sit apart from the Lords in the fourteenth century, and its consent became necessary for taxation. Its power grew under the Tudors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">Stuarts</a>, and it was replaced by the House of Commons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> at the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1707.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-commons-of-england.jpg" alt="House of Commons of England" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_England_(1509-1554).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
